      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the total number of months that have passed since Hal first replaced the batteries in May and then find out the month when the 25th replacement will occur.\n\nThe calculation goes like this:\n\n1. **Initial Replacement Date:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Initial Replacement Date} = 5 \\text{ months}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculating the Number of Months for Each Subsequent Replacement:**\n   Each subsequent replacement occurs every 5 months. To find out how many full sets of 5 months fit into the 25 iterations, we perform the following division:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{25 \\text{ replacements}}{5 \\text{ months/ replacement}} = 5\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determining the Starting Month for the 25th Replacement:**\n   The 25th replacement will complete the 5 full sets of 5 months. Therefore, we need to add 5 years and 5 months to the first replacement month (May).\n\n   - Year part of the sum: \\(5 \\) years\n   - Month part of the sum: \\(5 \\) months\n\n4. **Specific Month Calculation:**\n   Since May is:\n   \n   - 5 months **forward** (i.e., moving forward in the sequence).\n\nGiven that starting from May:\n   - **Year contribution**: \\(5\\) years keeps May unchanged because counting conventions usually start at year 0 for simplicity in such problems.\n\n   - **Addition for months**:\n   \\[\n   May + 6 \\text{ months (since we are adding 5 months to May)}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Final Month Calculation:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Final Month} = May + 5 \\text{ months}\n   \\]\n\nSince May + 5 months will give us:\n   - **May** for the 5 months,\n   followed by **Juni** after six months combination, \n   but since we are considering only months, it falls into the next January.\n\nThus, upon summing up the months:\n    The date will change:\n    \\boxed{January | 26th Month}\n\\boxed{May~ 25^{th}}\n\nThus, the 25th replacement will occur on:\n\n\\boxed{January~ 26th}",
